\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The screen flickered and the surface of the globe reappeared,
 marked by a hyperbola slicing right through Prydz Bay.
 If that was the source,
 it put the event at twenty past three,
 fifty-two minutes before it reached Leeuwin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family typewriter
\series bold
What is coming out of Prydz Bay?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Oscar responded,
 "Nothing since three twenty.
 Davis stopped at three nineteen.
 Zhongshan,
 Progress and Bharati at three twenty."
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Antarctic telemetry drops.
 It drops for weather and it drops for satellite windows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I closed Oscar,
 the screen and laptop.
 I needed to share.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The table was cleared and Lehua back at her book.
 Tūtū was in her chair,
 and the light had gone out of the sky while I was in my room.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tutu turned and watched me emerge.
 She looked at me and asked,
 "How long?"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
"It isn't that kind,
 Tūtū.
 It's ice.
 Ice floats —
 all of it,
 all the time,
 it's already floating.
 It doesn't make a wave."
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
She looked at me a while longer and turned back to the window,
 where there was nothing new to see.
